# Introduction
\pagenumbering{arabic}
\setcounter{page}{1}

## Grant Proposal Funding

When resources are limited, decisions have to be made on how to allocate them. In research, funding agencies make these decisions by accepting or rejecting grant proposals. Agencies contact panels of experts which individually review, rank or score proposals, the best of which are funded and the rest rejected [ @harman_management_1998 ]. There is evidence for bias in this process. For instance, funding for innovative, risky or interdisciplinary research is less likely to be awarded than for conservative and conventional proposals [@guthrie_measuring_2019]. Moreover, evaluations from individual experts are known to be unreliable, even after panel meetings, making it hard to objectively assess which proposals are worth funding [@fogelholm_panel_2012]. Despite these shortcomings, there is no existing alternative to this expert evaluation system (expert peer review). To mitigate its downsides, statistical methods can be used to analyse the experts' evalutations in order to provide fair decision making.

Depending on the assessment system, the experts either make qualitative or quantitative statements about the quality of the proposals. This project will focus on statistical methods to analyse two quantitative expert evaluation systems:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Scoring: Where experts give grades to proposal on either a continuous or ordinal scale.
\item Ranking: Where experts rank all, or a subset of the proposals from best to worst. 
\end{enumerate}

<!-- The main difference between the two systems is that ranking directly compares the proposals against each other, and scoring assesses each proposal individually. Reformulated, this means that when ranked, the proposals are evaluated on a relative scale and when scored, they are evaluated on an absolute scale. 
-->


To analyse these data, multiple statistical models, both frequentist and Bayesian, already exist. In this thesis, I will first review and reproduce existing statistical methods for evaluating scoring data and ranking data. Then, I will unify the scale of these models' results in order to provide a comparison of their performance. Finally, I will provide steps that can be taken towards creating a Bayesian joint Hierarchical model combining Scores and ranks. We hypothesize that this model would enable a more precise analysis of object quality in comparison to the other models made for this purpose. This would improve the current decision making process and ultimately allow resources to be allocated more fairly.

