## Definitions

Many different notations and formulations are used in literature to refer to ranking and scoring data. For clarity, this section defines the notation that we will use in this thesis. It is inspired from Heyard et al. [-@heyard_rethinking_2022] and Xia [-@xia_learning_2019].

### Objects and Assessors

We define objects as the set of items that are ranked and scored by the assessors. Conversely, we define assessors as the people giving scores and rankings to the objects. In this thesis, the objects will be the grant proposals and the assessors the experts on the evaluation panel.

::: {.callout-note title="**Definitions: Alternatives and Assessors**" }

**Objects**  are a set of $n \ \epsilon \ \mathbb{N}$ items to be ranked or scored by assessors. In this thesis we will denote the set of objects as $\mathcal{O} = \{o_1, ..., o_n\}$, with a subscript $i \ \epsilon \{1,...,n\}$.

**Assessors** are a set of $m \in \mathbb{N}$ experts who give scores and ranks to the objects $\mathcal{O}$.  We will denote the individual assessors with a subscript $j \ \epsilon \{1,...,m\}$.

:::

### Scores 

Scores are obtained by having assessors give a grade to each object by estimating its quality. Grades are measured on arbitrary scales that are either ordinal or continuous. When scoring, assessors examine each object individually and compare them to their representation of an ideal object; their assessment of each object is assumed to be independent from all other objects (see Figure \ref{fig-rankscore}). In this thesis, we will use the convention that low scores represent high quality and high scores represent low quality. \      

::: {.callout-note title="**Definition: Scores**" }

**Scores** $S_j$ are a set of grades given to all of $\mathcal{O}$ by an assessor $j$. The score given to a specific object $o_i \ \epsilon \ \mathcal{O}$ by a specific assessor $j$ will be written $y_{i,j}$ with $i \ \epsilon \  \{1,...,n\}$ and $j \ \epsilon \ \{1, ..., m\}$.

**Scoring data** $S = \{S_1, ..., S_m\}$ are the observed sores given by all assessors.

:::


### Rankings

In contrast to scores, rankings are obtained by having each assessor directly compare each object against each other and order them from best to worst. The objects are therefore assessed on a relative scale; the assessment of each object is directly influenced by the quality of all other objects in the alternatives (see Figure \ref{fig-rankscore}). In this thesis, we will use the convention that the best rank is the 1st, and then, as the rank ascends, the quality of the objects decrease. 

::: {.callout-note title="**Definitions: Rankings**" }

**Linear orders** $\mathbf{\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{O})}$ are the set of all possible ranking orders of $\mathcal{O}$. For any pair of alternatives $o_i$, $o_l$ $\in \mathcal{O}$, we will denote $o_i$ $\succ$ $o_l$ if $o_i$ is preferred to $o_l$.


**Ranking order** $R_j \ \epsilon \ \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{O})$ is the ranking order given by a specific assessors $j \ \epsilon \ \{1,...,m\}$ to $\mathcal{O}$. The rank position given to an object $o_i \in \mathcal{O}$ by an assessor $j$ will be denoted $r_{i,j}$.

**Ranking data** $R = \{R_1, ..., R_m\}$ are the observed ranking orders given by all assessors.

