### Object Quality and Final Ordering 

The ultimate objective of our modelling will be to select the most appropriate subset of objects for funding, given a constraint, usually the available funding amount. To obtain this subset, we will need to make a final ordering of the grant objects. This will either be achieved, by taking a model's predicted true underlying ranking, or by estimating and sorting a latent quality variable for each object. Once the final ordering is achieved, a funding line can be introduced. The funding line represents the highest value of the latent quality (the minimal quality standard) necessary for a object to be funded, or, in the case where only a final ordering is available, the limit rank a object needs to achieve in order to be funded.


::: {.callout-note title="**Definitions: Quality and final ordering**" }
**Object latent quality** $\theta^{k}_i$ is the latent quality of an object $o_i$ according to a model $k$. In this thesis, we will use the convention that lower values of the latent quality variables represent higher object qualities.

**Final ordering** $\mathcal{F}_k(\mathcal{O}) \ \in \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{O})$ is the final ordering of the object $o_i \in \mathcal{O}$ according to model $k$.

**Funding line** In the cases where $\theta^{k}_i$ is available, the highest value of the latent quality $\theta^{k}_i$ possible for an object to be funded. In the cases where only  $\mathcal{F}_k(\mathcal{O})$ is available, the limit rank a object needs to reach in order for it to be funded. 
:::


## Modelling Approaches to Scoring Data and Ranking Data

Models for scoring data are very common in the literature. In general, it is assumed that the scores given to objects follow a given distribution, from which the parameters can be inferred. By interpreting these parameters, statements are then made about the scoring data (for example, regarding the spread of the scores or the expected values of the scores given to specific objects). As ranking data are in a special format, they require extra consideration when being analysed. Consequently, many different formulations and approaches exist to model them. In this section, we will discuss notions of how ranking data can be thought of, described and modelled.

### Pairwise Comparisons and Ranking

Ranking data can be thought of as a series of pairwise comparisons. This approach considers that the assessors simply compare many pairs of objects and generate a ranking from these comparisons [@marden_analyzing_2014]. The ranking data is then modelled through a series of parameters representing the probability of winning these pairwise comparisons. This description of ranking data is used in numerous models such as the Bradley-Terry model [@bradley_rank_1952] and the Plackett-Luce model [@plackett_analysis_1975]. This description of ranks comes at a cost as the number of parameters increases very fast for larger ranking models. 


### Distance-Based Ranking

In distance-based ranking, we assume that the data have a true underlying ranking, and that the assessors are imperfect judges which attempt to perceive and reproduce this ranking [@fligner_distance_1986]. The true ranking is perceived as a ranking order $L \ \epsilon \ \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{O})$, and the further the observed ranking from this true ranking, the less likely the ranking is to be in the data. There are many possible parametrisations of this distance. Using a distance-based model instead of a pairwise comparison model drastically reduces the amount of parameters to estimate when modelling. Unfortunately, the simple interpretation of the parameters from the pairwise comparisons is lost in distance-based models.



### Thurstonian Ranking {#sec-tuhrst}

Thurstonian ranking is another way to model ranking. As in distance-based ranking, we assume that the assessors are imperfect judges. However, in opposition to distance based ranking, the ranking data is not assumed to be generated based on a true underlying linear order. Instead, we assume the ranking data to be generated by a series of latent random variables representing each object's quality [@thurstone_law_1927]. According to Thurstonian ranking, each assessor observes a realisation of all the random variables and ranks them from highest to lowest based on their value (see Figure \ref{fig-thurst}).
 
\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{figures/thurst_rank.png}
    \caption{Example of Thurstonian ranking with three objects. The lower the score, the higher the quality. Each assessor observes realisations of the three objects' latent quality distributions. The order of the realisations determines the order in which the assessors rank the objects. In this case, the most common ranking observed will be $o_1 \succ o_2 \succ o_3$, the rankings $o_2 \succ o_1 \succ o_3$ and $o_1 \succ o_3 \succ o_2$ will also commonly be observed, and the other linear orders will very rarely be observed.}
    \label{fig-thurst}
\end{figure}





