They are 21 missing data points in the scoring data, this represents $7.5\ \%$ of the data, with no single object having more than three missing scores. The MCAR data, despite increasing the variability in object quality estimation do not create any systematic bias [@dey_consequences_2026]. The methods we use on scoring data in this thesis have no problems handling missing values and there is therefore little interest in imputating MCAR datapoints. In opposition, the MCI data are intentionally not collected from the assessor because they are known to be biased. Accordingly, imputating these values is also undesirable. We can therefore analyse the scoring data without imputating nor modifying the scoring data.



## Ranking Data

An overview of the ranking data can be found in Figure \ref{fig:rank_expl}, no significant bias was found in the ranking data. 

There are only 11 missing data points in the ranking data, five MCI, and six MCAR. The MCAR data are due to an assessor ignoring the ranking task, and because of this, we simply remove them from the dataset as these observations do not provide any information about the object qualities. However, due to the nature of the models we are using, the MCI data is problematic. The models assume that when an object has a missing values, it did not make it in the top-6 of an assessor. Reformulated, a missing value given by a specific assessor represents an object quality worse than the 6th ranked object according to them. In the case of MCI data, the missing value does not provide any information about the quality of the object. There is a need for data imputation. However no statistical imputation tool exists in literature to imputate linear orders or ranking data (!)

Therefore, we define our own imputation model for this thesis. For simplicity, and because only very few data are missing, we will use single imputation. We will use the median ranking of an object to imputate the missing values. This new rank, by definiton, introduces a tie in the ranking of the objects. In order to account for this tie, the ranks of all objects ranked below the imputated data point for each specific asessor were increased by one. This was done prior to model fitting for all objects. (insert figure maybe?)


