## Models using Scoring Data

### Model of Means {#sec-mean}

A first and straightforward approach proposed by Zehlike et al. [-@zehlike_fairness_2022] to assess the quality of each object is to use the mean of all the scores $y_{ij}$ obtained by an object $o_i$ as an approximation of this object's quality. In this case, the latent quality of an object $\theta_i^{\ mean}$ is estimated as follows:

\begin{gather*}
\bar{y}_i = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^m y_{ij}}{m}, \quad i = 1, ..., n \\
\bar{y} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^n \bar{y}_i}{n} \\
\hat{\theta}_i^{\ mean} = \bar{y}_i - \bar{y} 
\end{gather*}

Note that, by definition, high quality objects will have negative values of latent quality $\hat{\theta}_i^{\ mean}$. This model relies on many strong assumptions. First, it assumes that each assessor has the exact same scoring habits, and is well calibrated with himself and the rest of the group as (for example, that no assessor is stricter or more lenient than their peers). Second, it also assumes that each object is evaluated completely independently from all other objects. Moreover, unless the amount of assessors is extremely large, it cannot be used to provide any reasonable uncertainty estimates in its results [@kaplan_sample_2008]. We fit this model using a \texttt{R} script [@R_core].

The Model of means provides an estimate of the latent quality $\hat\theta_{i}^{\ mean}$ of the objects from the scoring data. Figure \ref{fig:mm} shows the latent quality estimates for the ten best objects according to this model. The model of means would accept objects x17, x19, and x25. Object x19 and x25 are tied for second place, and the difference in latent quality between the tied second place and the fourth place object is very low. The exact latent quality values for the ten best objects accoding to this model are provided in Table \ref{tab:mm}.

