### Bayesian Hierarchical Model

To alleviate the assessor callibration assumptions discussed in @sec-mean, Heyard et al. [-@heyard_rethinking_2022] proposed a Bayesian hierarchical model that includes parameters to model the different scoring behaviors of the assessors. This model still offers a latent object quality variable $\theta_i^{\ hier}$ based on the mean of the scores obtained by each object. It is defined as follows:

\begin{gather}
y_{ij} | \theta_i^{\ hier}, \lambda_{ij} \sim N(\bar{y} + \theta_i^{\ hier} + \lambda_{j}\ , \sigma^2) \\
\theta_i^{\ hier} \sim N(0,  \tau^2_{\theta}) \nonumber\\
\lambda_{j} \sim N(0, \tau_\lambda^2) \nonumber
\end{gather}


 With $\bar{y}$ being the mean score of all objects, and $\lambda_{j}$ the assessor-specific effect representing the differences in scoring habits and strictness. Moreover, as this model is Bayesian, we can obtain posterior distribution for $\theta_i^{\ hier}$. (TODO describe Taus consistent prior with both INLA and JAGS). This can be used to estimate credible interval of $\theta_i^{\ hier}$. This model makes use of the funding line and Heyard et al. [-@heyard_rethinking_2022] propose that all proposals that have a 50\% credible interval of $\theta_i^{\ hier}$ crossing the funding line should be considered in a lottery instead of being directly accepted or rejected. 


This model can be fitted using the \texttt{ERforResearch} package [@heyard_2026]. This implementation uses the \texttt{rjags}  [@rjags] framework in order to generate a MCMC sample of the posterior distributions of $\theta_i^{\ hier}$. We will use both this implementation, and a reimplimentation of the package using the Integrated Nested Laplace Approximation (INLA) framework [@rue_approximate_2009] to fit the model. More information on both these methods can be found in Annex \ref{mcmc} and Annex \ref{inla}. The MCMC Convergence diagnostic are available in Annex \ref{coda-hier}. A comparison of both these methods can be found below. (TODO comparison of models, TODO explain priors used in INLA and rjags)

