## Models using Ranking Data
\label{rank_mod}

### Plackett-Luce

The Plackett-Luce model is a model based on pairwise comparison. It assumes that each object $o_i \in \mathcal{O}$ has a fixed probability of coming in first place when compared with the other objects in the set $\mathcal{O'} = \mathcal{O} \setminus \{o_i\}$ [@plackett_analysis_1975]. This probability is estimated by assigning a weight $w_i \in \mathcal{W}$ for each object $o_i$. The higher the value of the weight, the more preferred the object. The relationship between the probability of a specific object $o_{l}$ coming in first place and the weights $\mathcal{W}$ is defined as follows:

\begin{gather} 
\mathbb{P}[r_{l} = 1| \mathcal{W}] = \frac{w_{l}}{\sum_{i = 1}^{n}w_i} \label{decision-luce}
\end{gather}

The Plackett-Luce model considers each ranking order $R_j$ as a sequence of decisions made using Equation (\ref{decision-luce}). A formulation of the likelihood of this model can be found in Annex \ref{lik-Luce}.

In this method, the weights $\mathcal{W}$ are the quality estimates of the objects. In order to follow the convention that lower latent quality estimate represent higher scores, we will transform them as follows:
\begin{gather*}
\theta_i^{\ pl-lu} = (1 - w_i)
\end{gather*}
We then estimate the final ordering of the objects by sorting the weights $\mathcal{W}$ in ascending order. To estimate the weight parameters, we  fit a Plackett-Luce model using an R implementation by Turner et al. [@turner_modelling_2020]. 

