### Mallows

The Mallows model is a distance-based ranking method which considers a true underlying ranking order $R^* \in \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{O})$ and assumes the assessors are imperfect judges trying to reproduce this ranking [@mallows_non-null_1957]. The probability of observing a specific ranking order $R_j \in \mathcal{L}(\mathcal{O})$ is defined as:


\begin{gather*}
\mathbb{P}[R_j| R^* , \eta] = \frac{\exp(-\eta \ \ d(R_j, R^*))}{\psi_{\mathcal{O}}(\eta)} \\
d(R_j, R^* ) = \sum_{i,l = 1}^n \mathbb{1}\{o_i \ \ {\succ}_{R_j}  \ \ o_l \quad \text{and} \quad o_l \ \ {\succ}_{R^*}  \ \ o_i\} \\
\psi_{\mathcal{O}}(\eta) = \sum_{\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{O})}  \exp(-\eta \ \ d(R_j, R^*))
\end{gather*}
where $\psi_{\mathcal{O}}(\eta)$ is a normalizing constant, and $d(\cdot,\  \cdot)$ is the Kendall distance (minimum sum of adjacent swaps) between two linear orders. The notation $o_i \ \ {\succ}_{R_j}  \ \ o_l$ indicates that the object $o_i$ is ranked higher than $o_l$ in the true ranking order $R_j$, conversely $o_l \ \ {\succ}_{R^*}  \ \ o_i$ indicates that $o_l$ is ranked higher than $o_i$ in the true ranking order $R^*$. The $\eta$ parameter\footnote{In literature $\eta$ is often denoted as $\theta$. In this thesis, we use $\eta$, as $\theta$ is used to denote object quality.} represents a measure of similarity between the objects $\mathcal{O}$. A large $\eta$ implies that the distribution of the ranking data $R$ is closely concentrated around the true underlying rank $R^*$, while an $\eta$ of 0 implies that the distribution of the ranking data $R$ is uniform across the linear orders $\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{O})$. Using this method, the final ordering of the objects $\mathcal{F}_{mlw}(\mathcal{O})$ is $\hat{R^*}$. To fit the Mallows model, we will use an R implementation by Sorensen et al. [-@mallows_r].

The Mallows model provides an estimate of the true underlying ranking $\hat{R}^{*\ mlw}$ of the objects from the ranking data. Figure \ref{fig:mallows} shows the rank estimates for the ten best objects according to this model. This model would accept the objects x17, x25, and x19. The exact ranking values are provided in Table \ref{tab:mallows}.

