### Thurstonian Case V model

In Thurstonian case V modelling, the ranking order is assumed to be determined by drawing from latent random variables $z_{i}$ (as discussed in @sec-tuhrst). The ranking orders $R_j$ given by the assessors $j = \{1, ..., m\}$ is assumed to be determined by sorting a set of of $i = \{1, ..., n\}$ drawings of a random variables0 $z_{i}$ in descending order. In this thesis, we use the fifth case of the model as described in  [@thurstone_law_1927] to define the following:


\begin{gather*}
z_{i} \sim N(\mu_i ,\ \sigma^2)
\end{gather*}
where $\mu_i$ is a latent quality variable for object $o_i \in \mathcal{O}$ and $\sigma$ is the noise in the perception of this quality. The noise $\sigma^2$ is assumed to be the same in the perception of all object. This model is underdefined, we therefore add the following constraints: $\sigma^2 = 1$ and $\mu_n = 0$. A  formulation of the likelihood of the parameter $\mu$ using this constraint is available in [@maydeu-olivares_thurstones_2004]. We then define the latent quality variable as follows:
\begin{gather*}
\theta_{i}^{\ thurst} = -\mu
\end{gather*}
The final ordering of the objects by sorting the latent quality estimate $\theta_{i}^{\ thurst}$ in ascending order. In this thesis, we fit the Thurstonian model using an R implementation by Revelle [-@r_psych]. 

The Thurstonian case V model provides an estimate of the latent quality $\hat\theta_{i}^{\ thurst}$ of the objects from he ranking data. Figure \ref{fig:thurst} shows the rank estimates for the ten best objects according to this model. This model would accept the objects x25, x19, and x17. The exact latent quality estimates $\hat\theta_{i}^{\ thurst}$ are provided in Table \ref{tab:thurst}.


