## Combined Models

Combined models join the likelihood of both scoring and ranking models. We hypothesize that this can improve the accuracy in the prediction of the final ordering as both types of data are taken into account. 

### Mallows-Binomial

The Mallows-binomial model, as it's name suggests, is a model based on a combination of the Mallows model for ranking and a binomial model for scores. While the Mallows model is defined in the previous section (see Section \ref{rank_mod}), we have yet to define the binomial model for scores.

The binomial model for scores assumes that the scores given to the objects are ordinal, equally spaced and can be modelled by a binomial distribution. [@pearce_unified_2022] Reformulated from the perspective of the scoring data, each object has it's own Binomial distribution, and the assessors' scores given to each object are assumed to be independent observations of these distributions. The model can be formulated as follows:
\begin{gather*}
y_i | \pi_i, l \  \sim  \  Bin(\pi_i, l) \\
\mathbb{P}[y | \pi_i, l] = \binom{l}{y} \pi_i^x (1-\pi_i)^{l-x}
\end{gather*}
where scores have $l$ levels and $\pi_i$ is the underlying quality of an object $o_i$. The expected score of an object $o_i$ is $l \cdot \pi_i$.

Combining the Binomial model with the Mallows model provides the following probability distribution:
\begin{gather*}
\mathbb{P}[R_j, S_j | \pi, \eta]  = \frac{\exp(-\eta \ \ d(R_j, R^*))}{\psi_{\mathcal{O}}(\eta)} \times \prod_{i=1}^{n} \binom{l}{y_{ij}} \pi_i^{x_i} (1 - \pi_i)^{l-x} \\
\pi = [\pi_1, ..., \pi_n], \quad R^* = \text{Order}(\pi), \quad \eta > 0
\end{gather*}

This model incorporates $n + 1$ joint observations per assessor, with $n$ scoring observations and $1$ ranking observation. It jointly models the ranking and the scoring data by assuming the underlying ranking order $R^*$ is dependent on the ordering of $\pi$. Using this method we assume the final ordering of the objects is $\hat{R}^{*\ mbin}$. In this thesis, we fit the Mallows-Binomial model using an R implementation by Pearce [-@rankrate]. This implementation also includes a bootstrapped uncertainty estimate, which we provide in the results below.

The Mallows-binomial model provides an estimate of the true underlying ranking $\hat{R}^{*\ mbin}$ from both the scoring data and the ranking data.  This model would accept the objects x17, x25 and x19. The exact ranking values are provided in Table \ref{tab:mbin}.


