## Bayesian Estimations

In frequentist models, we define fixed parameters of interest, and then subsequently define methods to find a most likely value for these parameters using data (usually by using maximum likelihood estimation) [@held_likelihood_2020]. In Bayesian modelling, we also define parameters of interest. However, we assume that these parameters are not fixed, but follow distributions themselves. The focus is shifted from determining the most likely value for each parameter towards determining the most likely distribution for each parameter [@held_likelihood_2020]. In order to determine these distributions we use Bayes' theorem to obtain:
\begin{equation}
f(\symbf{\beta}| D) \propto  f(D | \symbf{\beta}) * f(\symbf{\beta}) \label{eq-bayes}
\end{equation}
where $\symbf{\beta} = \{\beta_1, ..., \beta_q\}$ are the parameters of interest and $D$ are the data used (see Annex \ref{bayest} for derivation). We denote $f(\symbf{\beta}| D)$ the posterior distribution; the distribution of the parameters after having taken the data $D$ into account, and $f(\symbf{\beta})$ the prior distribution; our belief about the distribution of the parameters before taking the data into account. The function $f(D | \symbf{\beta})$ is the likelihood of observing the data given the parameters of interest.

In the Bayesian hierarchical model and the joint hierarchical model (see Section \ref{methods}), there exists no closed form solution to the operation $f(D | \symbf{\beta}) * f(\symbf{\beta})$.  In order to obtain results, we use numerical approximation methods such as Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) and Integrated Nested Laplace Approximations (INLA). These two methods are described below. Details on these methods can be found in Annex \ref{mcmc}.


## Bayes' Theorem {#sec-bayest}
\label{bayest}
Baye's theorem is defined as follows:
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{P}[A|B] = \frac{\mathbb{P}[B|A] * \mathbb{P}[A]}{\mathbb{P}[B]}
\end{equation*}

In order to use it in modelling we reformulate it with $A$ as the parameters of interest and $B$ the data we have obtained. We define parameters of interest $\symbf{\beta} = \{\beta_1, ..., \beta_s\}$ and data $D$ to obtain:

\begin{equation*}
f(\symbf{\beta}| D) =  \frac{f(D | \symbf{\beta}) * f(\symbf{\beta})}{f(D)}
\end{equation*}


where $f(\symbf{\beta}| D)$ is the posterior distribution of $\symbf{\beta}$, $f(\symbf{\beta})$ the prior distribution of  $\symbf{\beta}$, $f(D | \symbf{\beta})$ the likelihood function of our model and  $f(D)$ a normalizing constant. $f(D)$ is normally estimated through marginalization:

\begin{equation*}
f(D) = \int^{\infty}_{-\infty} f(\symbf{\beta}, D) d\symbf{\beta} = \int^{\infty}_{-\infty} f(D | \symbf{\beta}) f(\symbf{\beta}) d \symbf{\beta}
\end{equation*}

This formulation of $f(D)$ in most cases does not have a closed form solution (unless the prior is conjugate to the likelihood). To avoid having to estimate it, it is often ignored and methods to estimate posterior distributions using only a proportionality constraint are used:

\begin{equation*}
f(\symbf{\beta}| D) \propto  f(D | \symbf{\beta}) * f(\symbf{\beta}) \end{equation*}



## Markov Chain Monte Carlo {#mcmc}

MCMC methods are based on two concepts, Monte Carlo simulations, and Markov chains. This section will first introduce these concepts individually, and then explain how they can be used in combination to estimate a posterior distribution, for example using Gibbs sampling.

### Monte Carlo {#sec-mc}

Monte Carlo simulations are a class of numerical methods that generate a series of random independent samples in order to estimate the solution to complex mathematical problems. These simulations are guaranteed, with a long enough simulation, to converge towards the analytical solution of the problem [@metropolis_monte_1949]. Converging is defined as progressively approaching the solution of a problem as the number of simulated iterations increases (for more on convergence see @sec-conv). In statistics, these simulations are often used to estimate integrals of density or probability mass functions, as well as generating posterior distributions.

Naïve Monte Carlo simulations work well on simple problems, but are heavily limited by the dimensionality of the problem they are solving. As dimensionality increases, both the size of the domain to explore using random numbers, and the number of iteration required for convergence increase exponentially. Figure \ref{fig:mc_normal} shows an example of two Monte Carlo simulation estimating the integral of a normal distribution for a small, and larger parameter space. 

<!-- However, most of this domain contributes only very little to the final solution. Adding a Markov-chain component to the Monte Carlo simulation enables us to use this property, and spend more time exploring and computing the high contribution domains while reducing the time spent on low-contribution domains of the functions we are analysing. This drastically reduces the computation time (Metropolis et al. 1953).  -->


