### Markov Chain

To improve the computational performance of Monte-Carlo simulations, we can use Markov-chains. Markov chains are series of numbers ${x^{(1)}, x^{(2)} , ..., x^{(q)}}$ in which any step $x^{(q)}$ depends, and only depends on the previous step $x^{(q-1)}$. By using Markov-chains in our Monte Carlo simulation, we no longer use random independent samples to generate solutions to our problems. This trick is used by MCMC to spend more time exploring the high contribution domains of the function to analyse. For example, in estimating the integral of a normal distribution, the domain which will contribute the most to the integral is between -4 and +4, values beyond this domain contribute little to the integral (see figure \ref{fig:mc_normal}). Only spending time in the "important" domains drastically reduces computation time [@metropolis_equation_1953]. In order to guarantee convergence of the MCMC towards the true solution despite the dependent samples, three more constraints are needed in the definition of the Markov Chain: Irreducibility, Aperiodicity and Detailed balance [@hanada_mcmc_2022].


::: {.callout-note title="**Definitions: Irreducibility, Aperiodicity and Detailed Balance**" }

**Irreducibility**: A Markov chain is said to be irreducible if there exists a transition between any pair $x$ and $x'$ in a finite number of steps $s$.

**Aperiodicity**: A Markov chain is said to be aperiodic, if the greatest common divisor of all the possible number of steps $\symbf{s} = \{s_1, s_2, ..., s_q\}$ between $x$ and itself is 1.  

**Detailed balance** The transition probability $\mathbb{P}[T(x \rightarrow x')]$ between any $x$ and $x'$ satisfy the following property:
\begin{gather*}
\mathbb{P}[x] * \mathbb{P}[T(x \rightarrow x')] = \mathbb{P}[x'] * \mathbb{P}[T(x' \rightarrow x)]
\end{gather*}

:::

### Metropolis Hasting algorithm {#sec-mh}

Markov-chains and Monte Carlo simulations can be combined into the Metropolis-Hasting algorithm [@hastings_monte_1970]. This algorithm is the basis of MCMC algorithms such as Gibbs sampling (see Section @sec-gibbs) and the Hybrid Monte Carlo (HMC) algorithm. In this section, we will first introduce the Metropolis algorithm and extend it to the Metropolis-Hasting algorithm. The next section will introduce Gibbs sampling which is used in the current implementation of the Bayesian hierarchical model. 

The Metropolis algorithm draws samples from a probability distribution $\mathbb{P}[x]$, provided that we have a function $f(x)$ that is proportional to its distribution. The Markov chain in the Metropolis algorithm is generated as follows.

::: {.callout-note title="**Definition: Metropolis algorithm**" }

**1. Initiate the Markov chain**: Choose a value for $x^{(1)}$, either randomly, or using prior knowledge about the target distribution. 

**2. Propose a candidate $\Delta x$**: Choose a real number $\Delta x$ randomly and propose $x^{(q+1)} = x^{(q)} + \Delta x$. The random generation process of $\Delta x$ must be symmetric around 0 in order to satisfy the irreducibility, aperiodicity and detailed balance conditions of the Markov-chain. 

**3. Perform the Metropolis test**: The candidate $x^{(q+1)}$ is accepted with a probability:
\begin{equation*}
\text{min}(1, \frac{f(x^{(q+1)})}{f(x^{(q)})})
\end{equation*}
If the candidate is not accepted a new candidate is proposed using step two of this algorithm.

**4. Repeat until convergence**: Repeat from step two until the distribution of the samples generated by the Markov-chain converges.

:::

This algorithm satisfies irreducibility, aperiodicity and the detailed balance of the Markov chain, and is therefore guaranteed to converge towards the target distribution, within a finite amount of iterations [@hanada_mcmc_2022]. 

Metropolis-Hasting algorithms, extends this algorithm by relaxing the constraints of detailed balance in generating new candidates. Instead, we allow candidates to be generated using any arbitrary function, as long as the series generated is still an irreducible and aperiodic Markov chain. To still maintain the detailed balanced conditions necessary for the chain to converge we modify the acceptance probability [@hastings_monte_1970]. This provides the following algorithm:

::: {.callout-note title="**Definition: Metropolis-Hasting algorithm**" }

**1. Initiate the Markov chain**: Choose a value for $x^{(1)}$, either randomly, or using prior knowledge about the target distribution. 

**2. Propose a candidate value**: Choose a new candidate value for the chain using an arbitrary function$f(x^{(q+1)} | x^{(q)})$.

**3. Perform the Metropolis test**: The candidate $x^{(q+1)}$ is accepted with a probability: 
\begin{equation*}
\text{min}(1, \frac{f(x^{(q+1)}) * f(x^{(q)} | x^{(q+1)}) }{ f(x^{(q)}) * f(x^{(q+1)} | x^{(q)}))}
\end{equation*}
If the candidate is not accepted a new candidate is proposed using step two of this algorithm.

**4. Repeat until convergence**: Repeat from step two until the distribution of the samples generated by the Markov-chain is converges

:::

The Gibbs algorithm and HMC algorithm are special cases of the Metropolis-Hasting algorithm with precisely specified candidate value functions $f(x^{(q+1)} | x^{(q)})$.


### Gibbs Sampling {#sec-gibbs}

Gibbs sampling is a Metropolis-Hasting algorithm used in order to estimate multivariate target distributions. Let us consider a target distribution $\mathbb{P}[\symbf{x} = {x_1, ..., x_r}]$ with a proportional distribution $f(\symbf{x})$ [@geman_stochastic_1984]. In Gibbs sampling, the candidate values $\symbf{x}^{(q+1)}$ are generated by using sequential conditional distributions:
\begin{gather}
x_a^{(q+1)} \sim f(x_a | x_1^{(q + 1)}, ..., x_{a-1}^{(q + 1)}, x_{a+1}^{(q)},  ..., x_{r}^{(q)})  \label{eq:gibbs} \\  
\text{For all } x_r \in \symbf{x} \nonumber
\end{gather}
This model is sequential and updated values at stage $(q+1)$ and values at stage $(q)$ are both incorporated together in order to obtain new candidate values. Moreover, the distribution of $x_a^{(q+1)}$ depends on all other variable $x_{-a}$ but not on $x_a^{(q)}$ itself.  Using such a generation process for $\symbf{x}^{(q+1)}$ provides the following Metropolis test in the Gibbs sampling algorithm:
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{P}[\text{acceptance}] = \frac{f(x_r^{(q+1)}) * f(x_r^{(q)}) | x_r^{(q + 1)}) }{ f(x_r^{(q)}) * f(x_r^{(q+1)} | x_r^{(q)})}
\end{equation*}
and by definition:
\begin{equation*}
\quad x_r^{(q+1)} \independent x_r^{(q)}
\end{equation*}
thus, 
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{P}[\text{acceptance}] = \frac{f(x_r^{(q+1)}) * f(x_r^{(q)}) }{ f(x_r^{(q)} * f(x_r^{(q+1)}) } = 1
\end{equation*}

Thus, the Metroopolis test is not needed in order to obtain the distribution $\mathbb{P}[\symbf{x}]$. Gibbs sampling was originally defined by Stuart and Donald Geman [-@geman_stochastic_1984]. However, we use here a simpler definition as formulated in Hanada and Matsuura [-@hanada_mcmc_2022]:


::: {.callout-note title="**Definition: Gibbs sampling algorithm **" }

**1. Initiate the Markov chain**: Choose a value for $\symbf{x^{(1)}} = \{ x_1^{(1)}, ... x_q^{1}\}$, either randomly, or using prior knowledge about the target distribution. 

**2. Propose a new value for each $\symbf{x}^{(q+1)}$**: Using the procedure described in equation (\ref{eq:gibbs})

**3. Repeat until convergence**: Repeat from step two until the distribution of the samples generated by the Markov-chain is converges.

:::

### MCMC for posterior distribution estimation

All the definitions in this section were described as estimating a distribution $\mathbb{P}[x]$ given an arbitrary proportional function $f(x)$. To obtain the posterior distribution as described in Equation (\ref{eq-bayes}). We reformulate the MCMC algorithm as follows:
\begin{gather*}
\mathbb{P}[x] = f(\symbf{\beta} | D) \\
f(x) = f(D | \symbf{\beta}) \cdot f(\symbf{\beta})
\end{gather*}
and we have:
\begin{gather*}
f(\symbf{\beta}| D) \propto  f(D | \symbf{\beta}) * f(\symbf{\beta}) \quad \quad \Rightarrow \quad \quad \mathbb{P}[x] \propto f(x)
\end{gather*}
Thus, we can simply sample from a likelihood $f(D | \symbf{\beta})$ and a prior distribution $f(\symbf{\beta})$, in order to estimate a posterior distribution $f(\symbf{\beta}| D)$.  


### Extra considerations

Trace plots are often used to assess the convergence and quality of a MCMC simulation. Trace plots are plots that shows the progression of the sampled parameter ($y$-axis) across the Markov chain's iterations (on the $x$-axis). On such plots, we can identify if models converge and the number of iterations taken in order to converge (see Figure \ref{fig-trace}). If the parameter is stably oscillating around a parameter value, we consider the model as converged.


As can be seen in the trace plot (see Figure \ref{fig-trace}), the first iterations of a MCMC simulation are heavily correlated with the Markov chain's starting value. Moreover, even when a Markov-chain converges, the samples it generates are autocorrelated. In order to limit the effect of these correlations on the solution provided by MCMC simulations, we use burn-in, skips and bins [@hanada_mcmc_2022]. Burn-in simply consists in discarding the samples in the Markov-chain that are deemed correlated with the starting value, to determine the number of samples to discard, trace plots are often used. Skipping and binning are used in an attempt to eliminate the correlation between samples after the burn-in period. Skipping (also called thinning), as it's name indicates, involves only taking a fraction of the observation produced by the Markov chain (for example 1 in 10 or 1 in 20 samples in the chain) in order to estimate the parameter of interest. If this skipping jump is longer than the autocorrelation structure, we obtain independent samples. Binning follows the same principle but instead of skipping samples, it groups them together into bins. The average of each bin is then taken as an independent observation. Multiple methods exist to determine the correct skipping or binning size, the most common of which is the Jackknife method [@efron_jackknife_1981]. A complete description of these methods is beyond the scope of this thesis. 


### Convergence  {#sec-conv}

In @sec-mc we state that given a long enough chain, a MCMC simulation will always converge towards the true analytical solution of a problem. This, while true, is a misleading statement. Indeed, as we are limited by computing capabilities, we are not always able to run a chain until it converges towards the solution of a problem. The easiest way to check if a simulation has converged, is by running it multiple times with different starting values (in MCMC this is denoted as multiple chains) and using a trace plot to visualise all chains (see Figure \ref{fig-trace}). If all chains converge to the same solution, the MCMC model can be considered converged. Despite its simple formulation, visual confirmation is often not sufficient in research. Therefore, there also exist a series of different diagnostic tools based on the variance within and between the chains, the most common of which is the $\hat{R}$ measure [@gelman_inference_1992]. A description of these methods is also beyond the scope of this thesis, a comparative study of these diagnostics can be found in  Roy [-@roy_convergence_2020].


In all cases where the model does not converge, the underlying reason is either that the model is misspecified or that the Markov chain is not long enough. A misspecified model can be modified and simplified. However, due to computing limitations, it is most often not possible to run a simulation for longer. In this case, the only option that remains is to make the simulation converge faster. This can either be done by simplifying the model, or, if the Metropolis-Hasting algorithm is used, by selecting a more appropriate function to determine new candidate values (see Section @sec-mh).


Despite all this freedom, sometimes models with multiple parameters and intricate distributions are too complicated to be solved using MCMC simulations, in these cases, we have to turn towards other estimation methods. In our case, as we are estimating the parameters of hierarchical Gaussian models, we will be turning towards the Integrated Nested Laplace Approximation [@rue_approximate_2009].



