## Generalisation of the normally distributed Thurstonian model

We modify the normally distributed Thurstonian model defined in Yao and Böckenholt [-@yao_bayesian_1999] to define the following Thurstonian model:

\begin{gather*}
z_{i} \sim N(\theta_{i}^{\ thurst},\ \sigma_{i}^2)
\end{gather*}
where $\theta_{i}^{\ thurst}$ is the latent quality variable for object $o_i \in \mathcal{O}$ and $\sigma_{i}$ is the noise in the perception of this quality. We can also write in a general setting:
\begin{gather*}
\symbf{Z_j} \sim N(\symbf{\theta},\ \symbf{\Sigma})
\end{gather*}

The vector of all scores $\symbf{Z_j} = \{z_1, ..., z_n\}$  drawn by an assessor $j$ can be defined as an $n$-variate normal distribution with mean $\symbf{\theta} = \{ \theta_1^{\ thurst}, ..., \theta_n^{\ thurst}\}$ and covariance matrix $\symbf{\Sigma} = diag(\sigma_1, ..., \sigma_n)$.


There exists no closed-form analytical solution to estimate the values of $\symbf{\theta}$ and $\symbf{\Sigma}$ from ranking data, an open form solution can be found in Annex \ref{lik-thurst}. However, this estimation procedure is very computationally intensive due to the high amount of integration required to obtain results. To solve this issue, parameter estimation can be done using Gibbs sampling [@yao_bayesian_1999 and @johnson_bayesian_2013]. No implementation of this algorithm was available online.


